%region 🔖️ForceKeys
% 🔑 semio / viz / layout / force — the options of the `force` layout. Every default is
% d3-force's own, so a bare call reproduces `d3.forceSimulation()` with `forceLink`,
% `forceManyBody` and `forceCenter`, ticked by hand.
%   iterations         (integer, default 300)   ticks; cost is O(iterations * (n^2 + m))
%   alpha              (number, default 1)      starting alpha
%   alpha-min          (number, default 0.001)  only used to derive the automatic decay
%   alpha-decay        (number, default -1)     -1 means 1 - alpha-min^(1/300), as d3 does
%   alpha-target       (number, default 0)      alpha decays towards this
%   velocity-decay     (number, default 0.4)    d3's public value; the multiplier is 1 - it
%   forces             (clist, default {link, many-body, center})
%                      the forces, applied in the order they are listed; the oracle has to
%                      register them in the same order for the comparison to mean anything
%   link-distance      (number, default 30)     rest length of a link
%   link-strength      (number, default -1)     -1 means 1/min(deg s, deg t), as d3 does
%   link-iterations    (integer, default 1)     passes of the link force per tick
%   many-body-strength (number, default -30)    charge of every node
%   distance-min       (number, default 1)      the many-body clamp, squared internally
%   distance-max       (number, default -1)     -1 means no upper cutoff
%   center-x/-y        (number, default 0)      where the centroid is pulled to
%   center-strength    (number, default 1)
%   collide-radius     (number, default 1)      one radius for every node
%   collide-strength   (number, default 0.7)
%   collide-iterations (integer, default 1)
%   x-target/-strength (number, default 0 / 0.1)   the per-axis springs
%   y-target/-strength (number, default 0 / 0.1)
%   radial-radius      (number, default 100)    the circle forceRadial pulls towards
%   radial-x/-y        (number, default 0)      its centre
%   radial-strength    (number, default 0.1)
%   seed               (number, default 1)      seed of the LCG behind jiggle()
% `theta` is deliberately absent: the many-body force is evaluated exactly, once per
% unordered pair, which is what d3 computes when theta is 0.
